WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.910 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i am  seven  every day before school  i turn on the tv and watch my favorite  science show for children it is nerdy  and sciencey and marvelous there are  colors and bubbles and explosions i love  it  now one day before school  i turned on the tv  it's not playing  very weird  day two  same thing  mommy  what's happening  my mom tells me  that the show's  been moved  it now plays when i'm  in  school  why would you play a children's show  when children are in school  how dare you adults are so  stupid  my mom says chill bro chill  she says that i should write a letter to  the tv channel  she doesn't think i'd write the letter  i am angry and i write the letter we  mail it in together  now  the next week  i turn on my tv  and  like magic my show is back  now  once again  every day before school i get colors and  bubbles and explosions and my life  is once again amazing  what did i learn  as a seven-year-old  anger  ignites action  ignites  change  as a young boy however  i was always told that anger  in malay is not part of malaysian  culture  good boys  literally being soft-spoken was  synonymous with being polite  talking back was bad raising your voice  was bad  anger  was bad  yet as i grew up  there were feelings  stronger deeper and harder to suppress  injustice incited indignation unfairness  unleashed umbrage  yet growing up in a semi democracy  meant that i couldn't fully  express these feelings  in here huh  you don't talk so much you talk okay the  government will come for you you watch  out  warn my parents my teachers  adults  no wonder the government had never lost  an election  people were angry yes  but people were even more  afraid  my friends let me ask you this  when was the last time you were truly  deeply  angry  and how did you react to it did you  embrace it did you let it move you or  did you distance yourself  from this feeling  i ask you this  because in my experience at stanford and  at harvard  i've found  that america isn't so different from  malaysia after all  at business school and at public policy  school there is this reticence  this distaste  this distancing from  anger  we the learned are intellectual we're  cerebral we are above this  fury is seen as uncouth wrath shameful  anger unbecoming  outrage  undignified  now  i think this is wrong  and this is why i'm telling you a story  about  an uncouth unbecoming andrew who used  anger  to change lives in profound ways  2018  my prime minister  steals 4.5 billion dollars  from my country  the the guardian calls it the world's  largest financial scandal your  department of justice calls it the  largest kleptocracy case ever  his cronies spend our money partying  with paris hilton and leonardo dicaprio  his stepson  spends our money to fund the wolf  of wall street  that's right  these guys stole malaysian tax money to  make a movie that glorifies how wall  street stole from american investors  you cannot make this up  how  dare  you  4.5 billion dollars stolen  malaysians are livid like never before  my prime minister knows that he cannot  suppress our anger any longer so he  decides to suppress  our votes  the economist proclaims stop thief  malaysian prime minister is about to  steal an election  financial times blares malaysian  election falls prey to gerrymandering  and as if this was not enough  he decides to hold polling day  on a wednesday  mid-week  not on a week end as is our tradition  this is a blatant attempt to stop urban  voters from traveling home to their  hometowns to vote  suppressing turnout  how  dare  you  furious  my friends and i decide to act  we found a movement to mobilize votes we  call it  go back to vote in malay  we ride the pulse of a nation we harness  the anger of thousands and turn it into  real tangible action  we are an overnight success  pulang mangundi becomes the top trending  hashtag on malaysian twitter  we are  winning  and then  and then 17 000 russian bots  attack us  17 000 russian bots  one month before this the world had  learned  about cambridge  analytica we had seen how russian  disinformation swayed the results of an  american election and against this  this this backdrop of global outrage and  global questions around the future of  democracy  we  wondered what russian mods were doing  in a malaysian  election  and then  the next day our friends  in high places told us that the secret  service  was watching us  we didn't know whether this was true  or what this meant but if these threats  were true  real or perceived  were our lives and liberty  at stake  this is how they get you  intimidated half of my team  wanted to shut our platform down  like so many before us we were angry  yet we were also  afraid  so  so we sat down over zoom  we named our fears  we told our stories and we were honest  to ourselves  and we listened  intently truthfully  and yet  i  pushed i challenge my friends to close  their eyes  and remember why  we started  in the first place  remember the 4.5 billion dollars stolen  remember the midday polling week to  suppress turnout remember the  gerrymandering  together we remembered  together we seethe  and together once again  we chose  to fight  my friends  in 2018  fulang magundi directly helped 10 000  voters travel home to vote  indirectly we energize online  mobilization  helping contribute to an 82 voter  turnout  we the people  kicked out a corrupt prime  minister we the people were angry we  took action and we won  my friends i am so proud  so proud to be a small part of the first  regime change in malaysian history  so proud to help end the reign of the  longest continuously ruling party in the  democratic world  angering knights action ignites change  plato tells us  that anger can be powerful  he calls it fumos  a special type of anger a deep  dissatisfaction a spirited zeal that one  can use and harness to overcome fear to  turn inaction  into action  i see  fumos  in the global reaction to stand with  ukraine  because we must stand up to mad men  i see fumos in the 20 million americans  who march for george floyd the largest  civic movement in american history  because black lives matter  i see thumos in ex gonzalez who awakened  the consciousness of millions of  students as a survivor of the parkland  high school shooting because we call bs  i see thumbos in greta tunberg who  shamed  global leaders into climate action  because how  dare  you  my friends  there's so much injustice in this world  and sometimes the only way out  is to be still  and listen to that voice in your heart  that pinch that fire in your soul  when i am  angry  i am closer to the truth  and when i am still  and listen to this truth and let this  truth move me  i  am  powerful  anger ignites action ignites  change  so i ask you this ladies and gentlemen  what are you  angry  about  [Music]

